title: The live build-failure test leaks a container on every run
updated: 2026-08-27T21:37:43Z
---

Found 2026-08-27 while cleaning up after a day of live runs.

The live subtest in t/stream_error.t builds an image whose RUN step exits 7,
to assert that a failed build is reported. A failed build leaves its
intermediate container behind, and nothing removes it.

Measured on the Docker daemon after a day of work: 14 containers, all
`Exited (7)`, all from the same intermediate image, timestamps spread across
every live run of the session. Docker's auto-generated names
(`priceless_driscoll`, `eloquent_rubin`, ...) because a build container is
created without a name.

Podman shows the same pattern for the same reason.

So every live-write run of the suite adds one, forever. Nobody notices
because the containers are exited and unnamed, and because the suite's own
cleanup only knows about containers it created by name via register_cleanup
-- a build intermediate is created by the daemon, not by the test.

To fix:
  - after the failed build, find and remove the intermediate. The build
    stream's own output carries the container id of each step, so it does
    not need a list-and-guess: parse it from the stream the test already
    reads.
  - check whether the successful-build tests leak too. They should not --
    a successful build removes its intermediates -- but measure rather than
    assume.
  - the same question applies to any other test that builds. Grep for
    `build` across t/ before calling it done.

Not urgent: exited containers cost a little disk and nothing else. But a
suite that leaks on every run trains everyone to ignore leftover state,
which is exactly how a real leak gets missed later.
